The simplest strategy and often the wrong default. One step, multiple threads share the same reader and writer.

**Configuration**

```java
@Bean
public Step multiThreadedStep(JobRepository repo, PlatformTransactionManager tx,
                              SynchronizedItemStreamReader<Order> reader,
                              ItemWriter<Order> writer) {
    return new StepBuilder("mt", repo)
            .<Order, Order>chunk(100, tx)
            .reader(reader)
            .writer(writer)
            .taskExecutor(new SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor("mt-"))
            .throttleLimit(4)
            .build();
}
```

**Requirements and gotchas**
- **Reader must be thread-safe** — most aren't. `JdbcCursorItemReader`, `FlatFileItemReader` maintain cursor state; wrap with `SynchronizedItemStreamReader`
- **Restart semantics weaken** — multiple threads advance the reader concurrently; `ExecutionContext` position may be inaccurate on restart
- **Output order is lost** — items written out of input order

```java
@Bean
public SynchronizedItemStreamReader<Order> safeReader(JdbcCursorItemReader<Order> delegate) {
    SynchronizedItemStreamReader<Order> r = new SynchronizedItemStreamReader<>();
    r.setDelegate(delegate);
    return r;
}
```

**Use when**
- I/O-bound step on a single machine
- Simple writer, restart-order not critical
- Quick win where partitioning would be overkill

**Avoid when**
- Strict restart correctness matters
- Reader cannot be made thread-safe
- Throughput requirements exceed what one JVM can do

The processor runs on a thread pool and returns a `Future`; the writer unwraps the futures and writes the actual results. The reader stays single-threaded — no thread-safety issues.

```java
@Bean
public AsyncItemProcessor<Order, Invoice> asyncProcessor(
        ItemProcessor<Order, Invoice> delegate) {
    AsyncItemProcessor<Order, Invoice> p = new AsyncItemProcessor<>();
    p.setDelegate(delegate);
    p.setTaskExecutor(processorPool());
    return p;
}

@Bean
public AsyncItemWriter<Invoice> asyncWriter(ItemWriter<Invoice> delegate) {
    AsyncItemWriter<Invoice> w = new AsyncItemWriter<>();
    w.setDelegate(delegate);
    return w;
}

@Bean
public Step processOrdersAsync(JobRepository repo, PlatformTransactionManager tx,
                               ItemReader<Order> reader,
                               AsyncItemProcessor<Order, Invoice> ap,
                               AsyncItemWriter<Invoice> aw) {
    return new StepBuilder("orders", repo)
            .<Order, Future<Invoice>>chunk(100, tx)
            .reader(reader).processor(ap).writer(aw)
            .build();
}

@Bean
public ThreadPoolTaskExecutor processorPool() {
    ThreadPoolTaskExecutor exec = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
    exec.setCorePoolSize(10);
    exec.setMaxPoolSize(20);
    exec.setThreadNamePrefix("async-proc-");
    exec.initialize();
    return exec;
}
```

**Why it works**
- Reader is still single-threaded — no thread-safety issues
- `AsyncItemProcessor` submits each item to the executor; returns a `Future<Invoice>`
- Chunk fills with `Future`s; `AsyncItemWriter` calls `future.get()` and writes actual results

**Use when**
- Processor makes HTTP calls, external lookups, CPU-heavy transformations per item
- Per-item latency dominates step duration

**Avoid when**
- Reader/writer are the bottleneck (still serial)
- Item processing has shared mutable state

A master step splits input into N slices via a `Partitioner`; each slice runs as an independent **worker step** with its own reader, processor, writer, and `ExecutionContext`.

**Local partitioning**

```java
@Bean
public Partitioner rangePartitioner() {
    return gridSize -> {
        long totalRows = 1_000_000L;
        long rowsPerSlice = totalRows / gridSize;
        Map<String, ExecutionContext> map = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < gridSize; i++) {
            ExecutionContext ec = new ExecutionContext();
            ec.putLong("minId", i * rowsPerSlice);
            ec.putLong("maxId", (i + 1) * rowsPerSlice);
            map.put("partition" + i, ec);
        }
        return map;
    };
}

@Bean
public Step partitionedMasterStep(JobRepository repo, Step workerStep,
                                  Partitioner partitioner,
                                  TaskExecutor partitionExecutor) {
    return new StepBuilder("master", repo)
            .partitioner("worker", partitioner)
            .step(workerStep)
            .gridSize(10)
            .taskExecutor(partitionExecutor)
            .build();
}

@Bean
@StepScope
public JdbcPagingItemReader<Order> workerReader(
        DataSource ds,
        @Value("#{stepExecutionContext['minId']}") Long minId,
        @Value("#{stepExecutionContext['maxId']}") Long maxId) {
    return new JdbcPagingItemReaderBuilder<Order>()
            .name("workerReader")
            .dataSource(ds)
            .selectClause("SELECT id, customer_id, amount")
            .fromClause("FROM orders")
            .whereClause("WHERE id BETWEEN :minId AND :maxId")
            .sortKeys(Map.of("id", Order.ASCENDING))
            .parameterValues(Map.of("minId", minId, "maxId", maxId))
            .pageSize(500)
            .rowMapper(new OrderRowMapper())
            .build();
}
```

**Key traits**
- Each worker has its own `ExecutionContext` with slice parameters
- Each worker is a full step — restartable, skip/retry, independent metrics
- Scales by `gridSize × worker throughput`
- Worker step **must be `@StepScope`** to receive the partition context via `@Value("#{stepExecutionContext['minId']}")`

**Remote partitioning**
- Workers run on separate JVMs; coordinated via messaging (AMQP, JMS, Kafka)
- Master publishes `StepExecutionRequest` messages; worker nodes consume, run, reply with status
- Use Spring Batch Integration's `MessageChannelPartitionHandler`

```java
@Bean
public MessageChannelPartitionHandler partitionHandler(
        MessagingTemplate template,
        QueueChannel replies) {
    MessageChannelPartitionHandler handler = new MessageChannelPartitionHandler();
    handler.setStepName("workerStep");
    handler.setGridSize(10);
    handler.setMessagingOperations(template);
    handler.setReplyChannel(replies);
    return handler;
}
```

**When to use remote**
- More than 10M rows, scaling beyond one JVM
- Cluster of worker pods on Kubernetes
- Workers needing dedicated machines (GPU nodes, high-memory instances)

**Partitioner strategies**
- **Range-based** — min/max ID per partition; simple for integer keys
- **Hash-based** — `mod(key, gridSize)`; uniform distribution for non-uniform IDs
- **Column-value** — one partition per region/tenant/file
- **File-based** — one partition per input file

Beware **data skew** — range partitioning over a dense first segment and sparse rest creates unequal work; hash usually gives better uniformity.

Spring Batch Integration provides `MessageChannelPartitionHandler` and helper classes for the master/worker handshake. The configuration mirrors local partitioning but replaces the `TaskExecutor` with a messaging channel.

**Master configuration**

```java
@Bean
public MessageChannelPartitionHandler partitionHandler(
        MessagingTemplate template,
        @Qualifier("replies") PollableChannel replies) {
    MessageChannelPartitionHandler handler = new MessageChannelPartitionHandler();
    handler.setStepName("workerStep");
    handler.setGridSize(10);
    handler.setMessagingOperations(template);
    handler.setReplyChannel(replies);
    handler.setPollInterval(2000);
    handler.setTimeout(300_000);
    return handler;
}

@Bean
public Step masterStep(JobRepository repo, Partitioner partitioner,
                       MessageChannelPartitionHandler handler) {
    return new StepBuilder("masterStep", repo)
            .partitioner("workerStep", partitioner)
            .partitionHandler(handler)
            .build();
}
```

**Worker configuration (separate JVM)**

```java
@Bean
public StepExecutionRequestHandler stepExecutionRequestHandler(
        JobExplorer jobExplorer, StepLocator stepLocator) {
    StepExecutionRequestHandler handler = new StepExecutionRequestHandler();
    handler.setJobExplorer(jobExplorer);
    handler.setStepLocator(stepLocator);
    return handler;
}

@Bean
public IntegrationFlow workerFlow(KafkaTemplate<String, byte[]> kafka,
                                  StepExecutionRequestHandler handler) {
    return IntegrationFlow.from(Kafka.messageDrivenChannelAdapter(
            consumerFactory(), "batch-requests"))
            .handle(handler, "handle")
            .channel(replyChannel())
            .get();
}

@Bean
@StepScope
public JdbcPagingItemReader<Order> workerReader(
        DataSource ds,
        @Value("#{stepExecutionContext['minId']}") Long minId,
        @Value("#{stepExecutionContext['maxId']}") Long maxId) {
    return new JdbcPagingItemReaderBuilder<Order>()
            .dataSource(ds)
            .selectClause("SELECT id, customer_id, amount")
            .fromClause("FROM orders")
            .whereClause("WHERE id BETWEEN :minId AND :maxId")
            .sortKeys(Map.of("id", Order.ASCENDING))
            .parameterValues(Map.of("minId", minId, "maxId", maxId))
            .pageSize(500)
            .rowMapper(new OrderRowMapper())
            .build();
}
```

**Shared JobRepository**
- Both master and workers connect to the same database holding BATCH_JOB_EXECUTION, BATCH_STEP_EXECUTION, BATCH_STEP_EXECUTION_CONTEXT
- Worker writes its `StepExecution` status to the shared repo
- Master reads worker status from the repo (fallback when reply messages are lost)

**Two communication patterns**
- **Request-reply via messaging** — master waits for reply messages on a dedicated channel
- **JobRepository polling** — master polls BATCH_STEP_EXECUTION; useful when reply channel is unreliable

Restart in remote partitioning is more nuanced than in local partitioning because the failure surface includes messaging.

**Restart semantics**
- A failed `JobExecution` retains all StepExecutions (master + each partition's worker) in the repo
- Restart launches a new `JobExecution` for the same `JobInstance`
- Master step re-runs — but only partitions with FAILED StepExecutions are re-published to the worker channel
- Workers process the re-delivered partition slices from scratch — idempotency required

**Idempotency strategies**
- **UPSERT writers** — `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE` for Postgres; `INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE` for MySQL
- **Idempotency keys in business records** — natural keys or hash of input
- **Outbox pattern** — write side effects to a table inside the chunk transaction; a dispatcher publishes them once
- **Defer side effects to a final step** — emails, API calls run only after partitioned import is fully complete

**Operational patterns**
- **Cleanup job** — a Spring Batch job that runs every N minutes, finds StepExecutions in STARTED status older than threshold, marks them FAILED
- **Replay tool** — operator runs a script that finds FAILED partitions and re-publishes their requests
- **Per-partition observability** — Micrometer metrics tagged with partition name; alert on outliers
- **Schema versioning** — partition's `ExecutionContext` may include a schema version; workers reject stale schemas to avoid running outdated business logic

```java
public class CleanupAbandonedExecutionsTasklet implements Tasklet {
    private final JobRepository jobRepo;
    private final Duration threshold;

    @Override
    public RepeatStatus execute(StepContribution contribution, ChunkContext ctx) {
        Set<JobExecution> running = jobRepo.findRunningJobExecutions("orderJob");
        Instant cutoff = Instant.now().minus(threshold);
        for (JobExecution exec : running) {
            for (StepExecution step : exec.getStepExecutions()) {
                if (step.getStatus() == BatchStatus.STARTED &&
                    step.getStartTime().toInstant().isBefore(cutoff)) {
                    step.setStatus(BatchStatus.FAILED);
                    step.setExitStatus(ExitStatus.FAILED.addExitDescription("abandoned"));
                    jobRepo.update(step);
                }
            }
        }
        return RepeatStatus.FINISHED;
    }
}
```
